>From: e0f1ztly@tuzo.erin (Bruno Fernandes)
>
>So then, it is fully permisable to rlogin to another machine that is part
>of CDF?  i.e. Towel, Killozap, Trillian, Jade, etc.  And it would also then
>be fully permisable to ftp as well from remote logins?  Why aren't users
>able to specify which machine they wish to connect to anymore?

When a local cluster implemented a similar setup a few years ago, 
it was because users always connected to their "favorite" machine
or the "fastest" (in their perception) system.  This, of course, 
resulted in a marked imbalance in system load, as the "popular"
machines were burdened by hordes of dialin users while the "un-
popular" systems saw little use.  Since all systems in the clus-
ter shared disk via NFS and supported the same software, this was
a rather silly waste of resources.  

>How many lines does CDF have this year?  I know the physical dialups are
>also used to provide UTLink, EPAS, etc. access.  I also know how busy the
>non-1200 numbers are.

Our modem lines are *constantly* busy.  I have major problems with
users tying my dialin ports to do nothing but telnet/rlogin to sys-
tems who dialin ports are all busy; as a result, users who need to
access *my* systems are left out in the cold.

This really seems to be a question of resource management; although
I don't use CDF systems, I doubt that any spite or malice is intended.

--Wes

