Date: Tue, 15 Feb 1994 09:47:12 GMT


Karl Kadie posted a response from Raynald Mercille, legal council
to McGill, defending newsgroup banning, below snipped down to
save space.

>By receiving a newsgroup and packaging it in a special folder
>for local users, the University is actually processing, editing
>and promulgating a newsgroup for local consumption, all of which
>is an activity which could be construed as publication.  I

        What distinguishes taboo information as a tiny fraction
of what is in banned newsgroups and that same information (not to
mention information violating other taboos) when it appears in,
say, alt.censorship? Surely, this must be "publication" under
this theory in every sense that it is when the same article
appears in alt.fan.karla-holmolka.

      The policy is plagued by arbitrariness. It is, of course,
wildly impractical to monitor all the the ways that said info
could leak in (or through) McGill mediated by USENET, leave alone
the Internet more generally. Fine. On grounds that seem to me
indistinguishable, the library washes its hands of responsibility
because it could not concievably discharge that responsibility,
i.e. monitor everything.

      In short, the "publication" theory does not defend the
policy because it fails to describe it. No one can say for sure,
but it is entirely possible that more copies of various banned
Homolka material will be "published" today through USENET plus
McGill Computers than were the day before the policy was put in
place. McGill is thus placed at risk since any authority made
aware of this ineffective result could fault McGill for failing
to carry out what it seemed to officially accept as it's
obligation, i.e. preventing the spread of material violating the
trial ban through its USENET+computer "publication facility".

      Points of clarification: exactly when and how under your
theory is the information "published" by McGill? It is said to be
OK to copy it to a terminal screen (which is a copy, even if not
a hard one). How about if a user prints the screen or saves to
disk or saves into volatile memory on the terminal device? Does
it matter whether user is dialed-in or in a McGill office? Is
there some difference between allowing any McGill user to make a
copy, and allowing some other university or private computer to
make a copy? Does it matter whether the programs are running
without immediate human supervision? with or without regard to
content? How about when a McGill user causes a program running at
McGlll to grab a copy from some computer elsewhere?

>First, you have yourself in the past expressed an extreme
>sensitivity to what you perceived as censorship.  There is no
>censorship in Vice-Principal Tavenas' decision, because the
>decision was not an imposition by Vice-Principal Tavenas of his
>own values on others:  rather Vice-Principal Tavenas did what
>had to be done by law.  Censorship is a process of editing
>material to deprive another from access to it.  Vice-Principal
>Tavenas' decision deprived even the supposed censor from having
>materials to edit: it was a blackout for all.

      This is 1984-speak. This unwillingness to use language in a
plain manner suggests bad faith. By such reasoning, the more
newsgroups (newspapers, magazines) choked off from ALL
downstream, the LESS censorship. So if Canada customs refused
admission to all foreign publications, they would thereby prevent
all censorship of such material. Please don't tell them.

>relation to suister universities.  The suggestion of McGill
>using its place in the USENET system to deprive other
>institutions of access and yet at the same time keep access is
>difficult to justify and in my opinion, would open McGill to an
>argument of inter institutional censorship.

      So McGill WOULD be engaged in "institutional censorship" if
other institutions were deprived of access but McGill was not.
How would you characterize making access harder for those with
lesser computer skills, but not those with greater? I am not
saying that Alan Greenberg (e.g.) does read the banned
information, but surely he has the skill to do so whenever he
wishes.

      Anyway, this logic seems very tortured. It is not
publication to leave it around to be read at McGill, (or maybe it
is depending on some unstated distinction between leaving it
around under USENET which "edits" et cetera versus somehow just
leaving it around unedited, unpromulgated) but to do this while
refusing to share would be "censorship". In short, to avoid being
censors, a key is to be thorough enough to get rid of otherwise
perfectly legal copies at McGill. MORE=LESS, anyone?

>prove to be their downfall.  This is an area of fascinating
>freedom yet unregulated by government.  Those who push that
>freedom to its limit may be inviting state regulatory
>intervention.

      Oh. The best way to "save freedom" is to make sure no one
exercises it. This again is the kind of double-speak that Orwell
identified with totalitarianism.  Turn it arouund. Those who
preempt freedom in fear of the state make it unnecesary for the
state to act.

>law.  By pulling the plug on the newsgroup globally, although he
>was forced to, Vice-Principal Tavenas ironically preserved the
>very freedom you now enjoy.  By contrast, an unbridled approach
>or a cunning evasion could possibly hasten the elimination of
>that freedom.

      At least this is called ironic. It reminds me of "to save
the village we had to destroy it".  Defend the policy as legal,
ethical, moral, whatever. But it is an offense to clear
expression to argue that in restricting free choice one is REALLY
protecting people from even worse repression. Does anyone else
feel a "chilling effect"?

      Simple question: do you favor or oppose government
restriction of information flow? You pose as an oppositionist. If
so, oppose the government or the judges. It is not helpful or
forthright to claim to support free expression and communication
while defending policies that do the very opposite. Better you
than the "real" censors? I'm not sure I'm grateful.

      Running through this memo is a fear of bad publicity and of
a goal of prudently heading off worse threats. These are
understandable objectives but they dodge a critical question. If
McGill is unwilling to take up the challenge and risk of offering
reasoned opposition, just who is going to do this? What I most
fear is that this policy of appeasing potential censors will tend
to disarm defenders of freedoms. Meanwhile, those attackers of
freedom that you claim to fear will be encouraged by your
lukewarm defense, or even learn from your clever ways of
describing censorship as the reasonable person's version of
freedom.

      For myself, I value the free flow of information. I would
prefer to see McGill reluctant rather than eager to lend support
to would-be regulators of information flow. If elements of the
judiciary or government desire elsewise, McGill can and should
speak out favoring clear, narrow, focused policies and should
resist all pressure to broaden or extend impediments to free
communication beyond the minimum required by the legal mandates
of other authorities.

      Case in point: broadly speaking, the libraries clearly
stated they could not reasonably be expected to inspect all
incoming foreign publications, and thereby seem to have deflected
pressure EVEN in such notorious instances as the "contaminated"
issue of the Washington Post. Compared with what enters the
libraries, USENET probably contains a higher proportion of
forbidden information even with the  most obvious newsgroups
censored, but government and judiciary  were left with the
impression that reasonable steps were available and had been
taken. This will NOT deflect future pressures but more likely
encourage them.

