The following online courses are non-degree credit courses offered by
The University of Western Ontario. The courses offered are:

        1) Connect With The World: Computer Communications
        2) Basics for Your Job Search
        3) Writing for Fun and Profit
        4) Navigating the Internet
        5) Shareware Secrets and Free Finds
        6) PC Exploration and Troubleshooting
        7) Introduction to Unix
        8) Programming the Bourne Shell

Short course descriptions follow.

For more information about these courses please feel free to contact:

        Mike Baycroft - baycroft@uwo.ca

or

        Catherine Blake - CatherineB@courier.ptce.uwo.ca

or call:

	(519) 661-3632

or fax:

	(519) 661-3799

These courses are conducted entirely online. By using an electronic
mail system on one of the University's host computers you will team up
with other students in a virtual classroom. Course handouts and
exercises will be delivered electronically. Class discussions will
take place online. You work from your home or office computer at your
convenience.

These courses run every time we have 10 or more current registrations.
We will contact you to confirm the start date, then we mail you a
personal start-up kit that contains the information you need to get
online. 

In order to take these courses you need either a PC, a modem,
communications software, and a phone line OR a connection to the
internet that allows you to telnet to a remote site. If you can telnet
to: 

   library.uwo.ca

and get a proper screen display, then you should be OK.

CONNECT WITH THE WORLD: PC COMMUNICATIONS

  Instructor: Mike Baycroft
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150 Canadian

This course will introduce you to basic communications terminology,
software, and hardware configuration.  You will learn how to transfer
files to and from remote computers and how to capture on-line
information. Learn how to use your home or office PC and modem to
connect you to the exciting world of on- line computer services.
Explore electronic mail, computer conferencing, and PC Bulletin Board
Services.  Discover new sources of information and software. Learn how
to transform your PC and modem into a business tool.

THE BASICS FOR YOUR JOB SEARCH

  Instructor: Janet Beretta
      Begins: Week of April 11
      Length: 4 weeks
        Cost: $120 Canadian

This online course is intended to provide the participants with an
understanding of the elements of the basic tools for the job search.
The material will cover basic resume preparation, review of differenct
types of resumes and their function, the use and effective composition
of cover letters, job interview preparation and research, and
interview skills. The instructor will also consult on individuals'
prepared resumes.

WRITING FOR FUN AND PROFIT

  Instructor: Mark Kearney
       Begin: April
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150 Canadian

Do you love to write, and envy people who get paid for it? Do you read
magazine and newspaper articles and think about how you would have
written them?

Prepare yourself to be a freelance writer/journalist with the
necessary tools to help get your articles published. This course
stresses practical work so be prepared to write. Examine differenct
kinds of non-fiction writing, learn how to grab the readers' attention
and to develop your own particular style. Inverviewing techniques are
discussed and put to practical use in writing articles. 

Your writing is evaluated regularly during the course. You will be
encouraged to complete at least one frelance article for
publication.  Journalistic experience is not necessary, but
enthusiasm is.

NAVIGATING THE INTERNET

  Instructor: Mike Baycroft
       Begin: March
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150 Canadian

Begin exploring the Internet today!  This course is an opportunity
for a guided tour of the international academic network.  Visit
Australia!  Correspond with someone in Norway!  Browse a library
catalogue in Mexico.  Discover special interest groups that span
international borders!

This course will introduce the new user to the basic tools used to
navigate the Internet: email, telnet, ftp, usenet, archie, gopher,
veronica, and wais. It will be of use to those who have a
professional or personal curiosity in computer networking as well
as those who would just like to meet other people around the world
who share the same interests.

SHAREWARE SECRETS AND FREE FINDS

  Instructor: Brian MacAskill
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150

Tired of paying the big bucks for programs you end up disappointed
in? It needn't be that way -- there's a wealth of great software
out there, and a lot of it's free (public domain) or free to try
(shareware). Join a shareware author on a tour of the best of
"non-commercial" software.

Find a great word-processor, a powerful database program, an
easy-to-use spreadsheet, and other useful gems. You'll learn the
ins and outs of software licensing: just what *can* you copy? What
can you keep? How much do you have to pay?

PC EXPLORATION AND TROUBLESHOOTING

  Instructor: Brian MacAskill
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150 Canadian

Discover some of the more common hardware and software failings of
PCs. Learn to use software that puts your PC to the test and makes
sure it's working efficiently. 

We'll also look at specialized software tools for:

- figuring out just what ports you have (and we'll tell what they're for!)
- determining whether or not your modem's up to scratch
- testing those pesky little RAM chips
- checking out floppy and hard drives
- pushing your video card to the limit
- and, of course, benchmarking your PC... is it really the fastest
  on the block?

You will have access to most of the software discussed in this
course.

INTRODUCTION TO UNIX

  Instructor: Mike Baycroft
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150 Canadian

Discover the beauty of unix! Unravel its web of mystery!

This course introduces the participant to the basics of unix.
Learn about the file system, basic file-maintenance, editing
files, and the command shell. Begin to explore the myriad of unix
commands and facilities that will profide you with a technical
solution to almost any computing problem.

PROGRAMMING THE BOURNE SHELL

  Instructor: Sandy Kechnie
      Length: 5 weeks
        Cost: $150 Canadian

Learn to write your own unix utilities and customize your unix
environment. Explore the vast storehouse of tools that will make
your life as a unix user a more enjoyable and productive one.
Beginning with your first tenuous steps of encapsulating a complex
unix command into a simple easy-to-use one, you will learn to
create sophisticated programs that accpt command line options,
work on multiple files, and even call other programs that you have
written. 

The prerequisite for this course is some knowledge of basic unix
commands, preferably an introductory course in unix and the ability
to use a text editor in the unix environment. Some exposure to
programming would be an asset.


