In article <127.2D6084D4@puddle.fidonet.org>,
Benson Schliesser <Benson.Schliesser@f18.n2250.z1.fidonet.org> wrote:
>>And, of course, if it were illegal for umich.edu to distribute it,    
>>soda.berkeley.edu would also be unable to do so.  One thing is clear:  
>>suppression of PGP is not happening for above-board legal reasons.   
>
>       Where the hell are you all getting this??? It's stated in the PGP
>documents quite clearly: It's only illegal to run it w/o permission from
>the patent holder and illegal to export it from the country.

Such statements are, at best, a minimum concession from the author(s). The
PGP documents aren't the law or the courts, and the authors are parties at
interest, not neutral arbiters.

Thus what they say is "illegal" is the minimum that's illegal, not the
maximum.

Questions of (U.S.) possession and distribution are, at best, unclear, and have
been argued extensively here over the past year or so.

Each of us is welcome to an OPINION on that matter, but it's important to
recognize that's all it is, unless or until a legal expert (or the courts)
tell us more. Some believe that under those circumstances caution is
indicated, while others, perhaps having less to lose, believe in pushing the
envelope as far as they can.

David
