SEND US INFORMATION YOU HAVE ON CULTURAL CENSORSHIP FOR INCLUSION IN A
MULTI-MEDIA ARCHIVE TO BE ACCESSIBLE BY MOSAIC!

WeU d like your help in developing a large scale project initiated by
Muntadas, an internationally renowned installation artist working in
collaboration with Randolph Street Gallery, a non-profit arts center in
Chicago, to produce The File Room. The File Room will be a hands-on,
interactive computer archive (employing the Mosaic program for the
Macintosh) of instances of cultural censorship around the world throughout
history. The archive will be installed as a public interactive artwork at
the Chicago Cultural Center, May 20 - September 4, 1994, and will be
globally accessible through Mosaic beginning  in mid-May.

The archive will begin with definitions of censorship gathered from a
number of sources. The diversity of these descriptions will function not
only to provide a breadth of viewpoints on what constitutes an act of
censorship, but also introduce the wavering nature of the concept. Who
defines censorship; those who censor, those who are the victims of
censorship; or an outside body? When and where has censorship been imposed
fiercely and when has it remained relatively quiet? What are the various
levels of censorship (outspoken, subtle, politically proscripted, tacitly
implied, etc.) The File Room will include cases of censorship in the areas
of visual art, music, dance, theatre, performance art, literature, etc. and
will not be limited to cases of censorship of individual artworks. One goal
of this project is to encourage people to re-examine their notions of what
censorship is and broaden its definitions beyond a well-publicized focus on
a specific work of art coming under fire. Some ideas to get you thinking:
the suppression of artistsU careers; bans on entire media or subject
matters in certain locations at certain times in history; self-suppression
by artists in fear of reprisals; denied or limited access to information on
cultural achievements by entire groups, or non-inclusion of such
information in RauthoritativeS sources compiled by majority
representatives; etc.

Visual, textual and sonic data on cultural censorship from around the globe
is being sought on instances of cultural censorship throughout history. In
addition, a bibliography will form a crucial part of the archive, citing
source material dealing with censorship in the cultural sphere.

WE ARE ASKING YOU TO HELP DEFINE AND CREATE THIS ARCHIVE. Though we are
engaging in some level of research, our hope is that the archive will be
developed chiefly through submissions solicited over the Net and through
the distribution of submission forms seeking snail mail and fax
submissions.

A version of the submission form follows below. The questions have been
developed in a way which we hope is open-ended enough to encourage you to
think about what you feel constitutes an act of cultural censorship. We ask
only that you answer each question as fully and concisely as possible so
that we may include all submitted information within a specific case
record.

1. Name of individual, organization or body censored or whose work was
censored.

2. Name of confronting bodies (both initiator of opposition/censorship and
others brought into this opposition).

3. Dates during which censorship action occurred.

4. Location of censorship action (as specific as possible: site, city,
state, region, country, etc.)

5. If the instance of censorship involves a specific artwork or group of
works, list the title, date of creation, media, dimensions/duration, and
content.

6. Short description of the case including action taken and results. (What
were the consequences of the action? Were there protests?, etc.) 

7. Source for your information. (If published, give full citation.)

If you have hard copies of documentation surrounding this case, please
snail mail or fax this information to the address/number below.

We are also seeking images and sound clips (on cassette or CD) related to
the case. If you have access to these, please snail mail them to the
address below.

If you know of other sources for information on cultural censorship, please
let us know of these sources.

Each submission will be credited in the archive (unless submitter wishes to
remain anonymous), so please include the following info. in all
correspondence:
Your name, address, phone/fax #, e-mail address 

Thanks for your participation in this project. We will be posting updates
to this newsgroup as the project progresses. Feel free to contact us at any
time for more information on The File Room.

