Malcolm McMahon <malcolm@geog.leeds.ac.uk> wrote:
>The essence of netiquette is curtosy and consideration. Kids tend to
>lack the judgement required.

When I was growing up in rural Kentucky, I ran into some of the most
arrogant age-based opinions you'd ever want to hear.  From the li-
brary aide who asked my father if he "really wanted someone this young
to be reading _One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich_" to the school-
teachers who became angry when I read my own books after finishing the 
work they assigned, I had to deal with the "you're not old enough" atti-
tude.  I remember being accused of getting help from my parents when I 
quoted the _New Republic_ in an 8th-grade history presentation; I remem-
ber being threatened with a failing grade when I corrected a high school
teacher in class.  Whose were the immature actions in these cases?

The idea that maturity (or intelligence) is an absolute function of age 
is ridiculous.  Would you care to estimate the age of Serdar Argic?  Dan 
Gannon? L Detweiler?  Does our inability to do so make a difference?  Your 
request for some sort of "age indicator" just proves the point that 
you cannot estimate one's age from one's words.

>How about allowing the kids to have net accounts but insisting that such
>accounts have an agegroup as part of the name? That way if there is too
>much hastle you can kill the little buggers in one go. Or maybe limit them
>to a certain quota of postings.

You talk about "courtesy and consideration," but you want a deadman
switch that will eliminate everyone in that class?  How, pray tell,
would that give "consideration" to each kid?

One last thought; do you have any idea how many kids under the age of
18 have *already* been participating?  Hordes of high school kids
have access to Usenet via PC BBSs, school sites and local universities
(don't assume that an address ending in .edu guarantees a college stu-
dent on the other end); it's a given that younger folks have been among 
us for several years.

I'm all for giving the kids access.  Now, I would like to have some 
means by which we could give feedback to the *educators* leading the
parade; several of us are already working on just such a facility.

--Wes

