On 2-6-94, you quoted parts of an article originally published in 
the _Globe and Mail_, which read as follows:

<other stuff deleted>

AA+Waterloo's decision to pull the plug on five of the dozens of sex
  +newsgroups has revived an angry debate that began at the
  +university in 1988 with an abortive attempt to ban a computer
  +service that carried racist jokes.  Administrators said at the
  +time that they would not interfere with free access to Internet.

The simplest way to deal with university administrators who choose to 
cover their butts at the expense of our freedom of speech is to fund 
private server sites so that people wishing to obtain a.b.p.e. 
files/GIF's can do so without fear of having the plug pulled on them 
because of conflicting interpretations of the law and disputes over the 
jurisdiction to act in the manner the University of Waterloo did.

As much as I personally dislike the University's patently knee-jerk 
reaction to files they believe contravene the obscenity provisions of 
the Criminal Code, I have to respect their decision. After all, the 
servers belong to the University, and to taxpayers. The fact 
that taxpayers' money goes to fund _all_ of UW's resources (and those 
of other Canadian postsecondary institutions), means that the servers 
are ultimately public property, if in an indirect way, and must be used 
for everyone's benefit and not for the interests of a certain set or 
subset of the university community, particularly where the files in 
question may be of little or no demonstrable usefulness in academic 
pursuits. Note carefully that I am *not* saying that 'little or no 
demonstrable usefulness' for any particular purpose excuses censorship.

I have no problem with porn in general. Hell, I've been known to view 
and enjoy the stuff on many an occasion. But I wouldn't ask, say, the 
University of Western Ontario's DB Weldon Library to carry the videos 
that a firm like _Adults Only Video_ does. The reasoning behind my 
argument is based on a simple distinction. AOV are a private firm who 
are in business to serve a particular clientele. It goes without saying 
that the clientele AOV serve are acting in a private, rather than 
public realm. On the other hand, universities find themselves 
rather squarely in the public domain. It is an unfortunate fact of life 
that the mainstream public are possessed of a mindset that does not 
readily tolerate the right of a minority to its freedom of self-
determination, particularly when it takes a form that the mainstream 
either find offensive or understand poorly.

What we have then, in such a case, is the rather classic struggle 
between majoritarian and minority interests. Unfortunately for us and 
the people at UW who have lost access to certain Internet resources, 
the majoritarian interests win out because the lawmakers and law 
enforcers either side with the majority or tend to listen to that 
polity more carefully. This is largely because their way of thinking 
or worldview, if you will, is geared to accepting the emotively 
reasoned arguments of a few alarmists as the only valid view and 
as the embodiment of majoritarian views. It is not merely 
coincidental that this pandering to a certain viewpoint provides a 
convenient springboard for the exercise of power, never mind whether or 
not such power is exercised in a just manner. Sometimes the mere 
ability to blindly exercise that power and garner broad-based 
political appeal is all that counts.

Again, at the risk of belabouring an earlier point- the best way to 
preserve access to a.b.p.e and some of the alt.sex newsgroups is to 
fund them directly as private individuals using private resources. Part 
of that funding should also include a reserve to provide a defence 
against the inevitable legal assaults by people like McKinnon 
and others who would seek to impose their moral standards on everyone 
else.

Steve
