There seems to be a fundamental misunderstanding among certain
McGill administrators about what constitutes censorship.
This would all be terribly amusing, if it did not directly
affect the academic mission of the University.

"Censorship" has a technical meaning defined by librarians:

	Censorship -- The change in the access status of material, made by a
	governing authority or its representatives. Such changes include:
	exclusion, restriction, removal, or age/grade level changes.

As quoted in the McGill Tribune (15feb94, p9), Vice-Principal F.Tavenas said:

	"What the people talking about censorship do not understand is that
	the best way to censor is to absolutely flood with information".

One wonders whether this reasoning is being applied to the budget
allocated for the McGill Libraries.
You see, to buy more books would constitute censorship.
Thus, to avoid censorship, the Library budget must be reduced so that
we do not overwhelm our library patrons with too much information.

In the limiting case, to eradicate censorship completely,
one could simply close all McGill Libraries.  Since there would be no
books to consider, no censorship would be possible!

