I have been following the various threads dealing with incidents of
"political correctness" on college campuses and campaigns against
professors who have made these so-called transgressions.  An incident
occured recently to a professor friend of mine which makes me wonder if
a number of these incidents aren't being fabricated by university
administrations to get rid of senior tenured faculty in an effort to cut
costs.

I will respect my friend's privacy and not reveal his name or the
college in which he teaches (especially since the case is still
pending).  However here are the details of his case:

He teaches at a medium-sized private college (not here at CMU, btw). 
The college, like a lot of private colleges, has been experiencing
financial problems recently.  This professor is over 65 but is still
quite active and has no intention of retiring at this time.  However,
the university has been after him on a number of occassions to retire.

He teaches economics and has taught a number of lectures where he's
demonstrated the effects of government programs (in particular,
Affirmative Action) using traditional economic theory.  He was recently
charged by a number of students with making "racially and sexually
insensitive" statements in class.  He's talked to his lawyer and he
feels his chances of beating these trumped-up charges are very good but
he's totally disgusted with the university for this cheap shot and with
his colleagues who have been unanimous in their sheep-like lack of
support for him.

Historically, the purpose of tenure was to provide protection for
faculty members so they would be free to espouse views that some people
might dislike without fear of retribution.  I'd very interested to learn
whether some of the other professors who have been involved in PC
incidents recently have backgrounds similar to the case I just
mentioned.  If so, it appears university administrations have finally
found a way around the protection of tenure.

Dave
