Your perception is correct.  As someone who has decried PC and its related
trampling of due process, free-speech and academic liberty, I receive a
large number of horror stories, many of them attested and compelling. 
Obviously, there are actual cases involving inappropriate BEHAVIORS, but
there are a growing number of cases in which politically incorrect beliefs
or personalities are being indicted under the guise of harrassment; it IS
a way in which colleagues can remove the protection of tenure selectively
and in partisan spirit.  It is far worse than the long remembered McCarthy
pressures at universities.  Further, a few PC administrators have sought
to use the notion of "cutting edge" or "conversant with new developments
in one's field" as a "competence" model of getting around tenure (the
pretense being that not being conversant with postmodern or feminist
theory in cultural fields is the equivalent of not being conversant with
quantum mechanics in physics in the early 20th century--an odd claim for
anti-positivists!).  These assaults are not going unchallenged, however,
and there are organizations of scholars and legal centers that are
fighting back by appropriate means.  The truly astonishing thing is how
few people notice these trends in the way that you have, and how few
faculty members are willing to become engaged in the struggle to preserve
essential dignities, decencies and freedoms in academic life.  Cheers, ACK
