Administrators frequently prefer to rid themselves of the strictures of tenure. 
It's protection in part depends on the willingness of the faculty to be united
in its opposition in the kinds of attempts you describe.  I know that in public
institutions we often have statutory protection (which is the case in
Wisconsin) plus the 1st Amendment/14th Amendment conncection which makes it
more difficulty for administrators to be as arbitrary as they would like to be. 
The private institution, though, does have the "contractural" or "property"
right protection which can be pursued.

Very frequently, the college or university that is made to realize that they
would have to provide a due process hearing, that the burden of proof would
rest on the administration, and that a subsequent judicial proceeding would be
likely is a good deterrent.  Your colleague, though, would have to show a
willingness to force the university through such processes.  Is the institution
unionized, or is there legal counsel available to faculty through voluntary
organizations such as AAUP or the ACLU?  In Wisconsin we can work with an
organization called The Association of University of Wisconsin Professionals.

I am a strong believer in things like affirmative action, challenging racism
and sexism, and diversity on college campuses.  Such principles are fully 
compatible with guarantees of academic freedom, freedom of speech and press,
and tenure.  Students and faculty who are offended by remarks made by faculty
and speakers should certainly challenge those remarks.  But they have a
responsibility to respect academic freedom. 

I would suggest that those who have coined the PC acronym are the right wingers
who have little respect for academic freedom.  Opponents of racism and sexism
should be the first to resist the "PC" attempts to infringe on academic freedom
because tampering with tenure and first amendment rights only strenghtens those
who would persist with racist and sexist practices. 

This may all sound nice in terms of ivory tower theory.  In practical terms, a
united and assertive faculty may be the needed protection.

